/**
@page TOPP_NASE_GraphSearch Experimental RNA graph filtering in NASE

@brief Bounded RNA mass tags for selecting precursor-matched candidates before full-spectrum scoring.

NucleicAcidSearchEngine offers five candidate modes:
- @p enumeration keeps the existing search.
- @p graph_audit scores all enumerated candidates and records whether a validated graph path supports each retained hit.
- @p graph filters precursor-matched candidates using RNA tags, then applies the existing full-spectrum score, coverage requirement, hit competition and native FDR calculation.
- @p graph_generate constrains variable-modification placement using precursor-mass completion bounds and graph-compatible residue alternatives, then applies the same graph validation and scoring as @p graph.
- @p graph_generate_audit compares generated candidates with exhaustive phase-one graph filtering, checks for missing or extra eligible variants, and scores the exhaustive reference. A candidate mismatch fails the run.

An example workflow uses FLASH to correct the MS1 precursor and retains legacy MS2 processing:
@code
NucleicAcidSearchEngine -in spectra.mzML -database targets_decoys.fasta \
  -out results.mzTab -id_out results.idXML \
  -precursor:correct_with_flash -preprocessing:deisotoping legacy \
  -candidates:method graph_audit -out_graph graphs.tsv
@endcode

Graph filtering is an experimental candidate-selection stage. The modification-aware
RNase digestion determines allowed products and consumes cleavage-sensitive modification
budgets before placement. The @p graph mode retains exhaustive placement as a reference.
The @p graph_generate mode extends ModifiedNASequenceGenerator's site compatibility
and site-subset enumeration with a predicate on partial placements. It preserves fixed,
pre-existing and terminal modifications and never spends the consumed budget again.

At each branch, mass bounds cover every possible completion, including negative
mass shifts and terminal groups. Unresolved internal sites retain all legal residue
labels, so an isobar is never chosen by mass alone. A branch can be rejected only
if no precursor in its mass envelope has a compatible tag or needs fallback.
These relaxed bounds may retain impossible completions; final exact precursor and
ion-series validation remain unchanged. The @p graph:max_placement_nodes bound
limits per-oligo exploration. Exceeding it discards partial output and restarts
exhaustive placement for that oligo. @p graph_generate_audit checks eligible
candidate multisets before theoretical-spectrum scoring, independently of PSM yield.

The theoretical ion annotations and residue labels are prepared once per
candidate/precursor-charge hypothesis and reused across matching spectra.
Validation stores dynamic-programming states only for observed ion matches.
It preserves the reference traversal, fallback limits and tag witness, while
avoiding repeated annotation parsing and dense per-spectrum state allocation.
Sorted theoretical groups also skip ions outside a conservatively expanded
observed m/z range. The full existing theoretical spectrum remains available
to the unchanged score and annotations.

Use @p graph:verify_matches to compare every prepared graph decision, fallback
reason and tag witness with the reference matcher. Any difference fails the
run. This can be combined with @p graph_generate_audit to check both placement
recall and matching equivalence in one search.

@section nase_graph_reuse Reused chemistry and scoring

The graph alphabet uses RibonucleotideDB records and the configured internal fixed
and variable modifications. Its edges retain every matching ordered residue
alternative instead of choosing a single label for isobaric masses. NASequence
labels, pre-existing modifications, parent mappings and cleavage-dependent
modification budgets remain part of NASE's existing candidate generation.

Paths are built in separate fragment-charge layers from the processed MS2 m/z
values; the scoring spectrum is not decharged or otherwise changed by the graph.
The graph uses residue increments including the RNA backbone mass. A step of two
residues bridges one missing fragment peak, with both residue orders retained
when mass alone cannot distinguish them. One- and two-residue anchors are
supported, including anchors after missing initial fragments.

A mass-difference path is only a hypothesis. Each accepted path must also match
one enabled a/b/c/d or w/x/y/z ladder in the candidate's annotated
NucleicAcidSpectrumGenerator output, with a consistent charge, direction and
fragment lengths. This reuses the existing ion and terminal masses. a-B ions
remain available for full-spectrum scoring and ambiguity resolution; they do not
seed the constant-residue-increment graph because their increments depend on
which bases and sugars are lost.

@section nase_graph_queries Compact graph and bounded tag queries

The residue/transition alphabet is prepared once and shared by all spectra.
Each spectrum stores only selected peak indices and a charge-layer DAG. Edges
reference every matching ordered residue alternative in the shared table. The
implementation does not materialize the combinatorial list of all graph paths.
Sequence tags are extracted on demand by bounded dynamic programming while
matching a candidate. Theoretical ion metadata confirms absolute mass, series,
charge, direction and terminal offset for every peak on a supported path.

Peak and charge caps deliberately select an evidence/hypothesis subset. Edge
counts bound each graph and the whole cache. A graph without an eligible tag, an
unsupported model, or a graph exceeding its storage bound uses enumeration.
Candidate queries have separate state/work bounds. A completed valid path is a
witness of support; an incomplete query without a witness uses enumeration.
This avoids rejecting candidates based on a partially explored graph or query.
The cache budget is applied in deterministic input order. None of these rules
inspect target/decoy status.

The @p out_graph table records spectrum IDs, selected peaks, cached vertices and
edges, alphabet size and graph-level fallback reasons. Retained idXML/OMS
matches record @p graph_candidate_status, @p graph_tag_length,
@p graph_ion_series and @p graph_fragment_charge. Supported matches also record
@p graph_start_ion and the @p graph_tag_sequence in 5'-to-3' orientation;
@p graph_candidate_method distinguishes audit from filtering. The reported tag
is a validated witness within the configured query bounds. Candidate-specific
fallback is recorded on each hit. Graph and fallback candidates are calibrated
together by NASE's existing FDR path. Nominal q-value yields must be assessed
with candidate recall and target/decoy behavior; ladder support alone does not
establish identification accuracy.
*/
